analysisWe need to produce an assert statement corresponding to the comment.

We have method add(int index, E element) which calls insertElementAt(element, index). The comment:

"@@@ If the index is greater than or equal to 0 and less than or equal to the initial size of the vector, then if the element is null, the element at that index in the vector is also null, or the element at that index equals the specified element."

Interpretation: After the add, if index is within [0, old size] inclusive, then the element at that index in the vector (i.e., get(index) after operation) is either null (if element is null) or equals the specified element. Actually phrasing: "if the element is null, the element at that index in the vector is also null, or the element at that index equals the specified element." Means: after insertion, at position index, the vector contains the element we inserted; if the inserted element is null, then the vector's element at that index is null; otherwise it's equal to element. So we need assert: (0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size())) => ( (element == null && this.get(index) == null) || (element != null && this.get(index).equals(element)) ). But we can simplify: this.get(index) == element (since null equality works with ==). In Java, compare null with ==, but for objects, we may want .equals. Use (element == null ? this.get(index) == null : this.get(index).equals(element)). Use implication.

Thus assert: (0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size())) => (element == null ? this.get(index) == null : this.get(index).equals(element));

Make sure to use \old for size before insertion. Use this.get(index) after operation. Also note that element may be null, so need proper handling.

Thus final code:

